      "title": "Flushing the toilet can release a ‘plume’ of breathable pathogens – and closing the lid won’t stop their escape",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/09/flushing-the-toilet-releases-pathogens-inhaled",
      "description": "<p>New research shows turbulent flushes create toilet-generated aerosols that can transmit legionella, influenza and norovirus</p><ul><li><p>Get our <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/email-newsletters?CMP=cvau_sfl\">breaking news email</a>, <a href=\"https://app.adjust.com/w4u7jx3\">free app</a> or <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/series/full-story?CMP=cvau_sfl\">daily news podcast</a></p></li></ul><p>Flushing toilets release particles into the “breathing zone” of adults and can remain suspended in the air for at least 20 seconds, a new study has found.</p><p>And closing the lid might not be enough to avoid a “plume” of potential pathogens.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/sep/09/flushing-the-toilet-releases-pathogens-inhaled\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
